From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 00:16:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08084/11362] A non-terminating induced engine could hang a
 scored run, and a signal fix would have hidden it

`dry_run_defects` EXECUTES LLM-written code and was bounded in transitions (limit=25) but not in
wall clock. On 2026-07-31 the best-of-N generation loop sat in state R at 32% CPU for 13 minutes
with no open socket and both GPUs idle, spinning inside the dry run of ft09 candidate 5. The
completion had already arrived, so no HTTP timeout applied, and the agent's wall budget is
checked BETWEEN actions rather than inside one. The shipped induce path reaches the identical
code via LocalGGUFProposer.generate -> _engine_defects -> validate_engine_code, so this was never
harness-only: on a scored submission it is the whole run.

THE OBVIOUS FIX WOULD HAVE MADE IT WORSE. A signal alarm gets SWALLOWED -- the dry-run loop wraps
every engine call in `except Exception` because the exception IS the observation it wants, and
`_engine_defects` upstream catches broadly and returns [], which means ACCEPT. So SIGALRM becomes
either a spurious `engine_raised` or a clean bill of health: a hang converted into a false CLEAN,
strictly worse, because a hang is at least visible. A signal is also main-thread-only and cannot
interrupt a tight loop inside a C-level numpy call. The bound is therefore a killable SUBPROCESS.

A TIMEOUT IS RETURNED AS A DEFECT, NOT RAISED. Raising would hit that same broad catch and be
converted into acceptance -- the exact failure being fixed. `engine_nonterminating` travels the
ordinary defect path, so the existing reject machinery handles it with nothing new. It is marked
neither repairable nor retryable: there is no exception text to feed back, because we never
observed the code end.

FOUND BY TESTING, NOT REVIEW: the first version pickled the caller's transition OBJECTS. This
module's contract is duck-typed and says so ("callers can pass test doubles"), so a locally
defined double died in the child with `AttributeError: Can't get attribute 'T'` -- and since a
non-zero child exit is reported as `engine_crashed_validator`, the ENGINE got blamed for an
artifact of crossing a process boundary. Now only the three attributes the dry run reads are
staged, via `_ShimTransition`. The test keeps its double local precisely to pin that.

Measured: a spinning engine is caught at the bound (3.0s in test) instead of never; a good engine
costs 0.44s; and every pre-existing observation survives the boundary intact -- `engine_raised`
KEEPS ITS EXCEPTION TEXT, which `repair_prompt_block` feeds back and which is the difference
between a veto and a fix.

Default 30s, chosen against what a real dry run costs (25 transitions of pure Python over 64x64
grids, milliseconds) rather than guessed -- three orders of magnitude of headroom, set far above
any honest run because the bound exists to catch non-termination, not to race slow code.
CARNOT_ARC_DRY_RUN_TIMEOUT_S=0 restores the pre-fix path exactly, and it calls the SAME function
the old code called rather than an approximation. A malformed value keeps the bound rather than
dropping it -- fail-safe is BOUNDED, since failing open silently reinstates the hazard.

RESIDUAL, STATED: if the subprocess cannot be started at all, this falls back in-process, i.e. to
the old hazard. That follows the same reasoning that justifies `_engine_defects`' swallow -- an
optional quality gate that can take down the path it checks is worse than no gate -- and only a
None return (infrastructure unavailable) takes that path. A timeout never does.

ALSO FIXED, because it was actively false: the module docstring still said "Nothing in the tree
calls validate_engine_code ... deliberately orphaned and allow-listed", a day after the wiring
landed and the allow-list entry was removed. That paragraph also set an obligation the wiring
change never discharged -- an integration test asserting validate_engine_code runs BEFORE
WorldModelVerifier.score on the same candidate. Both are discharged here rather than dropped: the
test drives the real proposer against a scripted server and asserts the call order. The order
matters because this module reports mechanical defects while the gate judges quality, so scoring
first grades broken code as merely WRONG -- which is what the 2026-07-30 audit found in four of
five rejections.

19 new tests. 80 passed across this module's suites. Mutation: disabling the bound by default
fails 5. Note for whoever runs mutations next -- my harness restored after each mutation but was
killed by a timeout mid-cycle and left `if True:` in shipped source; caught by an explicit
post-check. Put the restore in a trap, not after the command.

